-- title:
--   Spatial derivatives of elementary fields are elementary
-- statement:
--   A parametrized scalar field $f(p)(t,x)$, with parameters $p\in\mathbb R^d$, is elementary when $(t,x,p)\mapsto f(p)(t,x)$ is computed on $\mathbb R^{4+d}$ by a straight-line numeric program. The program is built from rational constants, coordinates, sums, products, negation, guarded inverses, $\exp$, logarithms of rationals and derivatives of $e^{-1/x}$. If $f$ is elementary, then for each $i\in\{0,1,2\}$ so is the field
--   $$
--   p\mapsto\partial_{x_i}f(p)(t,x).
--   $$
--
--   With the analogous statement for time derivatives, all mixed derivatives of elementary fields are elementary, hence effectively computable.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MathematicalPhysics/NavierStokes/BalancedTransport/ElementaryCalculus.lean#L126-L151

